WEBVTT

cue0138482
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.160
It's a pleasure to be here.

cue0138483
00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:36.720
Because the subject we're going to speak about is not only a window to penetrate the

cue0138484
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:49.759
history of Israel, but in a sense, it's a key to understand our own present and today.

cue0138485
00:00:49.759 --> 00:00:59.320
The name evokes a golden time in Jewish history.

cue0138486
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:02.040
We have time.

cue0138487
00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:18.080
The Jew was at the pinnacle of society, to be illustrious, to be intelligent, to be a

cue0138488
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:23.320
human of integrity, meant to be Jewish.

cue0138489
00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:35.559
To be Jew was a passport that opened the doors, the gates of the aristocracy.

cue0138490
00:01:35.559 --> 00:01:48.879
A Jew, a Christian, if he was not born of the aristocracy, was excluded from the corridors

cue0138491
00:01:48.879 --> 00:01:49.879
of power.

cue0138492
00:01:49.879 --> 00:01:59.360
And lo and behold, if you were a Jew, you had power.

cue0138493
00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.040
Because you had knowledge.

cue0138494
00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:11.039
Because you represented something that nobody else had.

cue0138495
00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:21.559
And thus, when we speak of Sepharad, we speak of a glorious moment.

cue0138496
00:02:21.559 --> 00:02:29.639
In a sense, not only for us Jews, but for humanity.

cue0138497
00:02:29.639 --> 00:02:34.679
What was Sepharadi culture?

cue0138498
00:02:34.679 --> 00:02:46.240
In a sense, Sepharadi culture has to do with the creation forged by four individuals.

cue0138499
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.360
Each one contributed something very special.

cue0138500
00:02:50.360 --> 00:03:06.559
But in a sense, whatever they contributed was not only the beginning of intelligence,

cue0138501
00:03:06.559 --> 00:03:14.199
but in a sense, I believe, is the key for post-modern man.

cue0138502
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:24.679
Today, we who were born in the modern period realize that there are certain limitations

cue0138503
00:03:24.679 --> 00:03:27.160
to modernity.

cue0138504
00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:35.240
We realize that modernity does not answer all of our problems.

cue0138505
00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:43.880
In a sense, all of us, we are seeking to be post-modern.

cue0138506
00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:50.679
My thesis is that to be post-modern is to be a Sepharadi.

cue0138507
00:03:50.679 --> 00:03:53.960
Let's see how.

cue0138508
00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:59.720
First, we're going to speak about one giant.

cue0138509
00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:01.720
Shalom.

cue0138510
00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:09.240
He was from Saragossa, Spain.

cue0138511
00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.800
We know very little of him.

cue0138512
00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:21.399
We know that probably he was born around the year 1045, more or less.

cue0138513
00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:24.920
What kind of profession he had, we're not sure.

cue0138514
00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:30.679
We know that as a vocation, because he did it le'shem shamayim, without taking salary,

cue0138515
00:04:30.679 --> 00:04:34.839
he was a dayan, he was a judge of the community.

cue0138516
00:04:34.839 --> 00:04:37.720
And he wrote a book.

cue0138517
00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:41.079
The title of the book, I don't like the translation in English.

cue0138518
00:04:41.399 --> 00:04:44.920
I don't care what the translation in English is.

cue0138519
00:04:44.920 --> 00:04:50.359
In English, it's translated as The Beauties of the Heart.

cue0138520
00:04:50.359 --> 00:04:57.399
In Hebrew, the translation is Chobot Hallevalot.

cue0138521
00:04:57.399 --> 00:05:00.359
He wrote in Arabic.

cue0138522
00:05:00.359 --> 00:05:06.920
And in Arabic, the title is Parayet ul-Gulut.

cue0138523
00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:10.200
What is parayet in Hebrew?

cue0138524
00:05:10.279 --> 00:05:16.440
Parayet is a literal translation of the word nesva, commandments.

cue0138525
00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:20.600
It means commandments of the heart.

cue0138526
00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:28.119
He came to an extraordinary revolution in the history of religion.

cue0138527
00:05:28.119 --> 00:05:38.359
We know that the Tora is a series of commandments, 613 to be precise.

cue0138528
00:05:38.519 --> 00:05:42.600
What are the objects of these commandments?

cue0138529
00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:49.480
Some of them have to do with our body, if we live, nice.

cue0138530
00:05:49.480 --> 00:05:55.480
Some of them have to do with our house, Mason's house.

cue0138531
00:05:55.480 --> 00:06:00.119
With the food, we shake it up.

cue0138532
00:06:00.119 --> 00:06:10.040
He discovered that the most important commandments of the Jewish faith,

cue0138533
00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:16.359
they have as an object our own hearts.

cue0138534
00:06:16.359 --> 00:06:21.880
There is parayet ul-gulut, the unresolved commandments,

cue0138535
00:06:21.959 --> 00:06:31.480
whose task is to metamorphose the heart of man.

cue0138536
00:06:31.480 --> 00:06:38.279
To be a Jew doesn't mean to be a pagan who puts tefillin.

cue0138537
00:06:38.279 --> 00:06:45.720
It doesn't mean to be a heathen who puts a mezuzah in the house.

cue0138538
00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:51.959
To be a Jew is to be a human being that he is transformed,

cue0138539
00:06:51.959 --> 00:06:57.559
he develops, he grows to a higher level,

cue0138540
00:06:57.559 --> 00:07:02.519
because he is the subject of nesvot.

cue0138541
00:07:02.519 --> 00:07:06.519
He does nesvot on himself.

cue0138542
00:07:06.519 --> 00:07:11.959
How?

cue0138543
00:07:11.959 --> 00:07:14.839
The most important.

cue0138544
00:07:14.839 --> 00:07:18.200
Shemaʿ ʾAdonai Eloheinu Aloha ʾEḥad.

cue0138545
00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:22.679
There is one God.

cue0138546
00:07:22.679 --> 00:07:28.200
But what does it mean that there is a God?

cue0138547
00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:36.839
It means a different level of human understanding.

cue0138548
00:07:36.839 --> 00:07:49.880
It means an evolution, higher up in the way we perceive and organize our thoughts.

cue0138549
00:07:49.880 --> 00:07:57.640
It means that you see the connection between the diverse phenomena

cue0138550
00:07:57.640 --> 00:08:01.160
taking place in nature.

cue0138551
00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:10.679
It means that you understand that there is a plan, that there is a purpose.

cue0138552
00:08:10.679 --> 00:08:19.079
Shemaʿ ʾAdonai Eloheinu Aloha ʾEḥad is impossible to fulfill

cue0138553
00:08:19.079 --> 00:08:26.359
unless you elevate yourself to certain category.

cue0138554
00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:29.880
Let me tell you something.

cue0138555
00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:46.840
A pagan in Greece was unable to connect between the simple fact that the wind blows

cue0138556
00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:50.119
and lo and behold, there is rain.

cue0138557
00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:56.919
Polyphemist means that every phenomenon in nature is separate.

cue0138558
00:08:56.919 --> 00:08:59.159
It rains, yes.

cue0138559
00:08:59.159 --> 00:09:00.280
It blows the wind, yes.

cue0138560
00:09:00.280 --> 00:09:04.440
But there is no connection between things.

cue0138561
00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:08.280
Mashi maruach omorida geshem.

cue0138562
00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:14.119
We say in the tequila, God blows the wind and has the rainfall.

cue0138563
00:09:14.119 --> 00:09:19.320
It's a new stage in human development.

cue0138564
00:09:19.320 --> 00:09:25.559
It constitutes an elevation of the human mind.

cue0138565
00:09:25.559 --> 00:09:29.239
Yes, as a Jew, I believe in evolution.

cue0138566
00:09:29.239 --> 00:09:34.280
And I believe that man, humankind to be more precise,

cue0138567
00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:39.479
is capable of evolving and becoming better and better.

cue0138568
00:09:39.479 --> 00:09:44.119
Take another response.

cue0138569
00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:45.559
Osum nishema.

cue0138570
00:09:45.559 --> 00:09:49.400
Be'ahatai ʾAdonai ʾElohekha b'chol me'odekha b'chol m'odekha b'chol me'odekha.

cue0138571
00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:53.960
You shall love God.

cue0138572
00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:59.640
To love God means to be happy.

cue0138573
00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:03.159
To enjoy the world.

cue0138574
00:10:03.159 --> 00:10:07.479
B'chol me'odekha b'chol mitda omidda she'omodet la.

cue0138575
00:10:07.479 --> 00:10:10.919
Everything that God does, I appreciate it.

cue0138576
00:10:10.919 --> 00:10:12.520
Say thank you.

cue0138577
00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:16.039
Say how wonderful.

cue0138578
00:10:16.039 --> 00:10:21.559
This is not a miṣva that a person could perform automatically.

cue0138579
00:10:21.559 --> 00:10:26.840
It is an existential transformation.

cue0138580
00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:32.679
Yes, sometimes we go to the movies.

cue0138581
00:10:32.679 --> 00:10:37.719
We see beautiful scenery.

cue0138582
00:10:37.719 --> 00:10:43.719
And we see the natives by the beaches and the roads,

cue0138583
00:10:43.719 --> 00:10:46.840
basking in the sun.

cue0138584
00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:50.280
How happy they are.

cue0138585
00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:54.119
But the truth, anthropologists tell us,

cue0138586
00:10:54.119 --> 00:10:55.880
that those natives are not happy.

cue0138587
00:10:55.880 --> 00:10:57.559
You know why?

cue0138588
00:10:57.559 --> 00:11:04.599
They don't have the intelligence to appreciate what they have.

cue0138589
00:11:04.679 --> 00:11:08.919
Ve'ahavtai ʾAdonai ʾElohekha,

cue0138590
00:11:08.919 --> 00:11:14.599
it means you appreciate what you have.

cue0138591
00:11:14.599 --> 00:11:17.159
You have to be happy.

cue0138592
00:11:17.159 --> 00:11:25.400
Not happy, you cannot fulfill the miṣva of loving God.

cue0138593
00:11:25.400 --> 00:11:28.280
Why?

cue0138594
00:11:28.280 --> 00:11:31.000
Take another miṣva.

cue0138595
00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:35.320
Ve'ahavtai l're'achah kamocha.

cue0138596
00:11:35.320 --> 00:11:37.000
Yes.

cue0138597
00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:45.159
You shall love your fellow human being as you love yourself.

cue0138598
00:11:45.159 --> 00:11:47.719
Fellow human being?

cue0138599
00:11:47.719 --> 00:11:50.760
He's a stranger.

cue0138600
00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:53.880
I don't know him.

cue0138601
00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:59.320
Maybe he harbors some ill to me.

cue0138602
00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:03.640
He has a better color than I do.

cue0138603
00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:07.640
I don't like the way he looks at me.

cue0138604
00:12:07.640 --> 00:12:14.599
To love another human being means that you yourself

cue0138605
00:12:14.599 --> 00:12:16.760
have to be lovable.

cue0138606
00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:20.520
That you don't mistrust people.

cue0138607
00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:28.359
That you understand that every single person, Jew or Gentile,

cue0138608
00:12:28.359 --> 00:12:31.960
pious or less pious, we're all pious.

cue0138609
00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:33.880
More or less.

cue0138610
00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:37.960
We all have a function.

cue0138611
00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:44.840
Just like the universe, all things are interrelated.

cue0138612
00:12:44.840 --> 00:12:50.280
Whether we want it or not, we all belong to one thing.

cue0138613
00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:52.440
Planet thing.

cue0138614
00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:53.479
Yeah.

cue0138615
00:12:53.479 --> 00:12:54.359
Planet Earth.

cue0138616
00:12:54.359 --> 00:12:55.320
Yes.

cue0138617
00:12:55.400 --> 00:13:00.200
In Rosh hashana, we say, kol va'eh ʿolam.

cue0138618
00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:03.320
Every member of the universe is in the same boat.

cue0138619
00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:12.760
So here we have, in my opinion, the most important book

cue0138620
00:13:12.760 --> 00:13:19.239
of ethics written in Spain.

cue0138621
00:13:19.239 --> 00:13:21.400
Judaism has commandments.

cue0138622
00:13:21.960 --> 00:13:26.679
The most important, most revolutionary commandments

cue0138623
00:13:26.679 --> 00:13:31.960
are the commandments that you make on your heart.

cue0138624
00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:35.960
If you change your heart with a thought,

cue0138625
00:13:35.960 --> 00:13:39.559
you accomplish something.

cue0138626
00:13:39.559 --> 00:13:46.679
If you don't, we say, amen, yes, we're thinking, we're praying,

cue0138627
00:13:46.679 --> 00:13:49.799
our lips are moving, and so on.

cue0138628
00:13:50.679 --> 00:13:51.159
It's fine.

cue0138629
00:13:51.159 --> 00:13:53.960
It's good.

cue0138630
00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:55.239
But it's not as good.

cue0138631
00:13:58.359 --> 00:14:01.479
Hobot, alev abod.

cue0138632
00:14:01.479 --> 00:14:04.440
Farai ul-kudru.

cue0138633
00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:09.640
This was the first ray of sun.

cue0138634
00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:14.119
Second, yehuda hal-levi.

cue0138635
00:14:14.679 --> 00:14:15.640
Yehuda hal-levi.

cue0138636
00:14:20.119 --> 00:14:23.719
What does it mean, yehuda hal-levi?

cue0138637
00:14:23.719 --> 00:14:31.880
Well, we have thousands and thousands and thousands

cue0138638
00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:35.640
of angry episodes in our history.

cue0138639
00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:36.140
Tragedy.

cue0138640
00:14:39.239 --> 00:14:39.739
Expulsions.

cue0138641
00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:47.179
We forgot that you lost the sense of aesthetics.

cue0138642
00:14:49.979 --> 00:14:57.979
You pray, and it is a purely devotional enterprise.

cue0138643
00:14:57.979 --> 00:15:02.619
Beauty, not for us.

cue0138644
00:15:02.619 --> 00:15:07.580
We are in Galut, singing.

cue0138645
00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:12.940
We hear the Gentiles, they sing.

cue0138646
00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:16.940
But yehuda hal-levi, he was not the first,

cue0138647
00:15:16.940 --> 00:15:22.940
but he was the greatest, brought the experience of aesthetic

cue0138648
00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:26.460
into Judaism.

cue0138649
00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:32.460
Through his poetry, he showed that to be Jewish

cue0138650
00:15:33.179 --> 00:15:43.179
is not only a matter of the mind, of performing,

cue0138651
00:15:43.179 --> 00:15:46.780
the Jew has to learn how to sing.

cue0138652
00:15:46.780 --> 00:15:57.500
If you cannot sing, you are not developing yourself as a Jew.

cue0138653
00:15:57.500 --> 00:16:05.500
Poetry, through yehuda hal-levi, became an integral part

cue0138654
00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:07.500
of the liturgy.

cue0138655
00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:14.940
Instead of praying to God, we began to sing to God.

cue0138656
00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:24.619
And song, aesthetics, became the earmark

cue0138657
00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:27.659
of the synagogue in Spain.

cue0138658
00:16:27.659 --> 00:16:35.500
Our prayers in Yom Kippur, our prayers in Yoseph Shonam,

cue0138659
00:16:35.500 --> 00:16:40.380
our prayers in Sukkot, our prayers of song.

cue0138660
00:16:40.380 --> 00:16:41.820
But there is something more to this.

cue0138661
00:16:45.179 --> 00:16:52.859
In the past, when we read our prayers,

cue0138662
00:16:52.859 --> 00:17:03.500
the prayers were written by King David, Elin, Solomon,

cue0138663
00:17:03.500 --> 00:17:08.300
Shir ha-Shirim, and Shekhereset HaGedolah

cue0138664
00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:12.619
in the Persian period, Kamilah.

cue0138665
00:17:12.619 --> 00:17:17.180
But the contemporary Jew was not permitted

cue0138666
00:17:17.180 --> 00:17:22.099
to sing with his own voice.

cue0138667
00:17:22.099 --> 00:17:30.180
A revolution, a revolution in the history of Jewish liturgy,

cue0138668
00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:36.020
through yehuda hal-levi, contemporary man

cue0138669
00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:44.619
writes his own thoughts, pours his thoughts into song,

cue0138670
00:17:44.619 --> 00:17:46.819
into song to God.

cue0138671
00:17:46.819 --> 00:17:52.420
Yes, it's beautiful to sing the songs of the past.

cue0138672
00:17:52.420 --> 00:17:58.619
But a liturgical experience must have

cue0138673
00:17:58.619 --> 00:18:01.219
the present contemporary dimension.

cue0138674
00:18:01.219 --> 00:18:10.339
Yehuda hal-levi used poetry, not only to evoke Jewish religion,

cue0138675
00:18:10.339 --> 00:18:15.180
but he's the first person that used poetry

cue0138676
00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:19.339
to regenerate a political ideal.

cue0138677
00:18:19.339 --> 00:18:23.859
Sion, halotish, alilish, om, asirai.

cue0138678
00:18:23.859 --> 00:18:31.099
He starts singing the songs of Zion, of Erez Yiśraʾel.

cue0138679
00:18:31.099 --> 00:18:37.420
He used poetry to produce, 1,000 years later,

cue0138680
00:18:37.420 --> 00:18:41.140
a gentleman by the name of Theodore Herzl, Ben-Gurion,

cue0138681
00:18:41.140 --> 00:18:42.180
et cetera.

cue0138682
00:18:42.180 --> 00:18:45.020
He is the first Zionist.

cue0138683
00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:49.699
It wouldn't be because yehuda hal-levi.

cue0138684
00:18:49.699 --> 00:18:52.380
I believe that still there will be a Herzl.

cue0138685
00:18:52.380 --> 00:18:56.219
But we're taking maybe a little bit longer, you see?

cue0138686
00:18:56.219 --> 00:18:58.900
Maybe a little bit longer.

cue0138687
00:18:58.900 --> 00:19:02.180
Song, the power of song.

cue0138688
00:19:02.180 --> 00:19:07.260
That is the second ray of light in Sepharad.

cue0138689
00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:15.339
I will speak now about a third Zionist,

cue0138690
00:19:15.339 --> 00:19:22.739
about the same time, a junior to yehuda hal-levi.

cue0138691
00:19:22.739 --> 00:19:29.300
And this is a gentleman by the name of Rabbi Abraham

cue0138692
00:19:29.300 --> 00:19:29.780
Ibn Nazar.

cue0138693
00:19:32.819 --> 00:19:35.660
Who is Abraham Ibn Nazar?

cue0138694
00:19:35.660 --> 00:19:37.300
Well, he was born also in Spain.

cue0138695
00:19:37.300 --> 00:19:41.459
He was much younger than yehuda hal-levi.

cue0138696
00:19:41.459 --> 00:19:47.339
And apparently, so it seems, his son, Isaac ʾIbn ʿEzraʾ,

cue0138697
00:19:47.339 --> 00:19:51.859
apparently, so it seems, married the daughter of yehuda hal-levi,

cue0138698
00:19:51.859 --> 00:19:53.780
or in the Sepharadi language of our center,

cue0138699
00:19:53.780 --> 00:19:55.819
were consuegros, you know?

cue0138700
00:19:55.819 --> 00:19:59.459
And they were good friends.

cue0138701
00:19:59.459 --> 00:20:02.619
But it was a difficult thing.

cue0138702
00:20:02.619 --> 00:20:09.619
First of all, Rabbi ʾIbn ʿEzraʾ was a commentator to Ezra.

cue0138703
00:20:09.619 --> 00:20:11.900
What happened?

cue0138704
00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:18.260
We read the Bible for 1,000 years.

cue0138705
00:20:18.260 --> 00:20:19.459
And we knew how to recite it.

cue0138706
00:20:22.979 --> 00:20:27.180
And we knew how to translate it.

cue0138707
00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:31.380
And we knew how to translate the Bible into Arabic.

cue0138708
00:20:31.380 --> 00:20:34.699
But what happened was that we did not

cue0138709
00:20:34.699 --> 00:20:40.060
know the grammar of Arabic, of Hebrew, I'm sorry.

cue0138710
00:20:40.060 --> 00:20:42.979
Hebrew was a language, sacred.

cue0138711
00:20:45.819 --> 00:20:49.699
But what are the rules of grammar?

cue0138712
00:20:49.699 --> 00:20:53.459
How do you conjugate a verb?

cue0138713
00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:57.260
What's the difference between a noun and an adjective?

cue0138714
00:20:57.260 --> 00:21:02.619
What's the etymology of Hebrew?

cue0138715
00:21:02.619 --> 00:21:08.420
And the truth is that people knew the translation by rote,

cue0138716
00:21:08.420 --> 00:21:10.780
but not by understanding.

cue0138717
00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:12.300
And that created a problem.

cue0138718
00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:13.099
I'll tell you why.

cue0138719
00:21:15.859 --> 00:21:21.060
In a way, the Moorish culture in Spain

cue0138720
00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:26.020
was a little bit of a problem in America.

cue0138721
00:21:26.020 --> 00:21:29.420
In Moorish Spain, you could be any religion you want.

cue0138722
00:21:29.420 --> 00:21:30.260
You could be Jewish.

cue0138723
00:21:30.260 --> 00:21:31.219
You could be Christian.

cue0138724
00:21:31.219 --> 00:21:32.060
You could be Muslim.

cue0138725
00:21:32.060 --> 00:21:35.300
Nobody bothers you.

cue0138726
00:21:35.300 --> 00:21:44.219
But when it comes to education, the best school,

cue0138727
00:21:44.219 --> 00:21:50.900
the best way in which you write was Arabic.

cue0138728
00:21:50.900 --> 00:21:58.140
And therefore, naturally, Jews knew Arabic.

cue0138729
00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:00.579
And they went to school.

cue0138730
00:22:00.579 --> 00:22:07.020
And they had a higher level of Arabic knowledge.

cue0138731
00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:10.339
When they went to the Talmud Tora,

cue0138732
00:22:10.339 --> 00:22:19.180
the knowledge of Hebrew was not at the same level

cue0138733
00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:22.660
than the knowledge of Arabic.

cue0138734
00:22:22.660 --> 00:22:31.099
And this was an imbalance, not because Hebrew was inferior,

cue0138735
00:22:31.099 --> 00:22:34.260
but simply because there was no means

cue0138736
00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:40.900
to be able to understand the intricacies of that language.

cue0138737
00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:44.660
And if you do not understand Hebrew as well as you understand

cue0138738
00:22:44.660 --> 00:22:50.739
Arabic, naturally, you're going to tilt

cue0138739
00:22:50.739 --> 00:22:54.619
in favor of the Muslim culture.

cue0138740
00:22:54.619 --> 00:22:59.219
And whether you're aware of it or not,

cue0138741
00:22:59.219 --> 00:23:04.819
other systems of thought invade your mind.

cue0138742
00:23:04.819 --> 00:23:12.939
To be able to be a full-fledged Jew in a pluralistic society,

cue0138743
00:23:12.939 --> 00:23:20.819
you must know your Bible as well as you know your Shakespeare.

cue0138744
00:23:20.819 --> 00:23:28.060
And ʾIbn ʿEzraʾ is the first Hebrew commentator

cue0138745
00:23:28.060 --> 00:23:33.780
who annotates on every word of the Bible

cue0138746
00:23:33.780 --> 00:23:38.380
the philological, etymological, and grammatical meaning.

cue0138747
00:23:38.380 --> 00:23:46.420
You cannot be a second-rate Jew in a first-rate society.

cue0138748
00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:53.540
And all of a sudden, the Jew understood the Bible,

cue0138749
00:23:53.540 --> 00:24:00.819
excuse me, not as a pharaoh, but as an intelligent reader.

cue0138750
00:24:00.819 --> 00:24:04.180
He understood the secrets of the Hebrew language.

cue0138751
00:24:08.540 --> 00:24:12.900
But there was another aspect of ʾIbn ʿEzraʾ.

cue0138752
00:24:16.380 --> 00:24:24.140
A teacher of mine, Mias Valicosa in Spain,

cue0138753
00:24:24.140 --> 00:24:44.060
published in 1943 a book in Latin in which ʾIbn ʿEzraʾ

cue0138754
00:24:44.140 --> 00:24:51.219
gives you a table concerning the rise and fall

cue0138755
00:24:51.219 --> 00:24:57.180
of all the constellations that could be visible at the time.

cue0138756
00:24:57.180 --> 00:25:00.420
Now, there's something very important.

cue0138757
00:25:00.420 --> 00:25:03.579
The part in the manuscript was called

cue0138758
00:25:03.579 --> 00:25:06.500
Tablas Astronomicas de la Vía Rámenes.

cue0138759
00:25:06.500 --> 00:25:08.459
Tables with all the rise and the fall,

cue0138760
00:25:08.459 --> 00:25:12.739
and when you see them, the stars disappear.

cue0138761
00:25:12.739 --> 00:25:18.500
Why is it so important, this manuscript?

cue0138762
00:25:18.500 --> 00:25:22.780
It's important for two reasons.

cue0138763
00:25:22.780 --> 00:25:25.260
First of all, we're reading in Latin.

cue0138764
00:25:29.099 --> 00:25:34.060
This is not a translation from the Hebrew into Latin.

cue0138765
00:25:34.060 --> 00:25:37.819
It is an original Latin translation,

cue0138766
00:25:38.660 --> 00:25:43.380
Latin composition by ʾIbn ʿEzraʾ.

cue0138767
00:25:43.380 --> 00:25:48.140
Since Mias Valicosa, many other works, all of them

cue0138768
00:25:48.140 --> 00:25:50.979
to do with science.

cue0138769
00:25:50.979 --> 00:25:54.900
For instance, here's a book on mathematics,

cue0138770
00:25:54.900 --> 00:26:00.579
one of the early books on mathematics written in Latin.

cue0138771
00:26:00.579 --> 00:26:03.459
Here is a book on the construction

cue0138772
00:26:03.459 --> 00:26:05.260
of the astrolobos.

cue0138773
00:26:05.260 --> 00:26:08.619
Astrolobos was a primitive machine

cue0138774
00:26:08.619 --> 00:26:11.219
that served to measure the height

cue0138775
00:26:11.219 --> 00:26:16.739
and the distance of stars, how you build an astrolobos.

cue0138776
00:26:16.739 --> 00:26:24.819
He had several books, also in Latin,

cue0138777
00:26:24.819 --> 00:26:28.540
to be able to calculate the hours and the days

cue0138778
00:26:28.540 --> 00:26:31.180
in which a particular star would be visible or not visible

cue0138779
00:26:31.180 --> 00:26:34.180
from different places on Earth.

cue0138780
00:26:34.180 --> 00:26:38.979
This last book was very important for navigation.

cue0138781
00:26:38.979 --> 00:26:43.060
At the time, the main way to navigate

cue0138782
00:26:43.060 --> 00:26:46.339
was through astronomy.

cue0138783
00:26:46.339 --> 00:26:50.500
He wrote a book in Latin on astronomy and the stars.

cue0138784
00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:54.500
So he wrote in Latin.

cue0138785
00:26:54.500 --> 00:26:57.420
And now we know that there was a reason why he wrote in Latin,

cue0138786
00:26:57.420 --> 00:27:04.500
because he was part of the scientific community

cue0138787
00:27:04.500 --> 00:27:06.500
in Western Europe.

cue0138788
00:27:06.500 --> 00:27:09.300
He was in England.

cue0138789
00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:11.579
And there, where he wrote his book on mathematics,

cue0138790
00:27:11.579 --> 00:27:14.060
say that it's one of the early books on mathematics

cue0138791
00:27:14.060 --> 00:27:16.819
that reached England.

cue0138792
00:27:16.819 --> 00:27:19.540
He was in Lucas, Italy.

cue0138793
00:27:19.540 --> 00:27:22.619
He was in Pisa, Italy.

cue0138794
00:27:22.619 --> 00:27:27.380
He reached all the way to the Middle East, to Egypt.

cue0138795
00:27:27.380 --> 00:27:34.579
He was one of the great disseminators of science.

cue0138796
00:27:34.579 --> 00:27:45.420
And here we have a person who is 100% Rabbi, a great poet,

cue0138797
00:27:45.420 --> 00:27:51.459
and one of the pioneer scientists of the Middle Ages.

cue0138798
00:27:51.459 --> 00:27:57.339
And throughout his life, he believed in number one,

cue0138799
00:27:58.300 --> 00:28:01.300
that science and the Tora are not only

cue0138800
00:28:01.300 --> 00:28:04.699
compatible with each other, but are

cue0138801
00:28:04.699 --> 00:28:07.420
indispensable to each other.

cue0138802
00:28:07.420 --> 00:28:11.939
The same God that wrote the good old book

cue0138803
00:28:11.939 --> 00:28:13.900
is the same God who created the universe

cue0138804
00:28:13.900 --> 00:28:15.739
and established the laws there.

cue0138805
00:28:15.739 --> 00:28:17.619
Nothing can contradict each other,

cue0138806
00:28:17.619 --> 00:28:23.900
because all were the creation of one single God.

cue0138807
00:28:23.900 --> 00:28:29.699
And therefore, for him, it was a matter of fact

cue0138808
00:28:29.699 --> 00:28:34.739
that Tora and Madar and science are just

cue0138809
00:28:34.739 --> 00:28:38.060
two sides of the same coin.

cue0138810
00:28:38.060 --> 00:28:41.780
But there's another thing that he believed.

cue0138811
00:28:41.780 --> 00:28:45.060
He was a scientist, and he believed

cue0138812
00:28:45.060 --> 00:28:50.140
that those who brought science into the world

cue0138813
00:28:50.140 --> 00:28:51.020
were not the Greeks.

cue0138814
00:28:54.540 --> 00:29:00.060
He said, maybe right, maybe wrong, I don't know,

cue0138815
00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:04.219
that they were the Jews, ʾAbraham ʾAḇinu,

cue0138816
00:29:04.219 --> 00:29:10.900
the one, the great scientist from Babylonia who brought

cue0138817
00:29:10.900 --> 00:29:17.420
science into Egypt, was the Jews at the time of Yirmiya,

cue0138818
00:29:17.420 --> 00:29:22.979
were exiled into the Hellenistic world,

cue0138819
00:29:22.979 --> 00:29:28.219
and then he brought science to the pagan world.

cue0138820
00:29:28.219 --> 00:29:29.939
Is it true?

cue0138821
00:29:29.939 --> 00:29:31.420
I don't know.

cue0138822
00:29:31.420 --> 00:29:36.180
But it's interesting that ʾIbn ʿEzraʾ believed that science

cue0138823
00:29:36.180 --> 00:29:37.380
is part of the Jewish mind.

cue0138824
00:29:37.380 --> 00:29:44.979
And in a sense, if you believe that there

cue0138825
00:29:45.020 --> 00:29:50.219
is one God author of everything, then you

cue0138826
00:29:50.219 --> 00:29:52.900
must postulate that there must be

cue0138827
00:29:52.900 --> 00:29:58.939
some harmony between the different phenomena.

cue0138828
00:29:58.939 --> 00:30:04.459
In other words, that there are scientific laws.

cue0138829
00:30:04.459 --> 00:30:10.380
Scientific laws means everything follows

cue0138830
00:30:10.380 --> 00:30:13.140
a mathematical system.

cue0138831
00:30:13.140 --> 00:30:13.640
Precise.

cue0138832
00:30:16.619 --> 00:30:25.140
And there is a passage, there is a verse in the Psalms

cue0138833
00:30:25.140 --> 00:30:34.380
saying that the stars, yes, misaperim kevod el.

cue0138834
00:30:34.380 --> 00:30:39.140
The word misaperim means to count.

cue0138835
00:30:39.140 --> 00:30:43.140
But in Hebrew, like in English, misaperim

cue0138836
00:30:43.140 --> 00:30:47.699
means not only to count, but to count, to count the stars.

cue0138837
00:30:47.699 --> 00:30:49.420
And if you believe misaperim is the count,

cue0138838
00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:55.140
the mathematical formula that controls

cue0138839
00:30:55.140 --> 00:30:59.660
the motions of all the stars, this

cue0138840
00:30:59.660 --> 00:31:05.699
means par, tell us of the glory of God.

cue0138841
00:31:05.699 --> 00:31:11.380
Reveal God as a mathematician.

cue0138842
00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:12.180
True?

cue0138843
00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:13.420
I don't know.

cue0138844
00:31:13.420 --> 00:31:17.060
I wanted just to present to you the mind

cue0138845
00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:20.339
of a great, great scholar.

cue0138846
00:31:20.339 --> 00:31:23.219
And there is a point that I must say.

cue0138847
00:31:23.219 --> 00:31:26.699
He was a superb poet.

cue0138848
00:31:26.699 --> 00:31:30.099
And still on Shabbat, yes, we all

cue0138849
00:31:30.099 --> 00:31:34.660
sing the Eshmere Shabbat, El Yishmere.

cue0138850
00:31:34.660 --> 00:31:39.140
And this is the poem that he wrote.

cue0138851
00:31:39.140 --> 00:31:44.420
It's time not to read the poem that he wrote.

cue0138852
00:31:44.420 --> 00:31:50.900
Ben Adama, are there any great mathematician

cue0138853
00:31:50.900 --> 00:31:52.979
who is also a great biblical scholar, who

cue0138854
00:31:52.979 --> 00:31:57.420
is also a poet, that 700, 800 years later still

cue0138855
00:31:57.420 --> 00:31:59.060
people sing his song?

cue0138856
00:31:59.060 --> 00:31:59.560
Yes.

cue0138857
00:32:03.060 --> 00:32:03.560
Last.

cue0138858
00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:07.060
Rabbi Moshe Bar-Maimon.

cue0138859
00:32:10.060 --> 00:32:14.060
Let's see what we had before.

cue0138860
00:32:14.060 --> 00:32:18.560
We had the miṣvot have to transform you.

cue0138861
00:32:18.560 --> 00:32:22.560
The miṣvot are dear to your heart.

cue0138862
00:32:22.560 --> 00:32:24.060
Or what are they about?

cue0138863
00:32:24.060 --> 00:32:24.560
One.

cue0138864
00:32:25.560 --> 00:32:26.560
Second.

cue0138865
00:32:26.560 --> 00:32:31.560
You're going to be ecstatic.

cue0138866
00:32:31.560 --> 00:32:36.560
Songs, feelings, beauty.

cue0138867
00:32:36.560 --> 00:32:39.560
Third, ʾIbn ʿEzraʾ.

cue0138868
00:32:39.560 --> 00:32:42.060
Science.

cue0138869
00:32:42.060 --> 00:32:44.560
Mathematics.

cue0138870
00:32:44.560 --> 00:32:47.560
Seeing the universe, the majesty of creation.

cue0138871
00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:58.560
But to survive as a Jew, we Jews believe in the law.

cue0138872
00:33:04.560 --> 00:33:12.560
But in order to have a viable legal system,

cue0138873
00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:13.560
you need a juridical system.

cue0138874
00:33:17.560 --> 00:33:26.560
A minister of justice, a judicial organization,

cue0138875
00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:32.560
a police under the judiciary who enforce

cue0138876
00:33:32.560 --> 00:33:33.560
the decision of the court.

cue0138877
00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:43.560
And we Jews had none of the above.

cue0138878
00:33:43.560 --> 00:33:47.560
So how can we observe the law?

cue0138879
00:33:47.560 --> 00:33:55.560
How can we follow the law if we don't know the law?

cue0138880
00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:59.560
Well, you say, it's the Rabbi.

cue0138881
00:33:59.560 --> 00:34:02.060
Fine.

cue0138882
00:34:02.060 --> 00:34:05.560
But the Rabbi is a human being.

cue0138883
00:34:05.560 --> 00:34:09.560
How do we know that the Rabbi is a human being?

cue0138884
00:34:09.560 --> 00:34:13.560
Well, it's not the Rabbi.

cue0138885
00:34:13.560 --> 00:34:15.560
But the Tora doesn't say this.

cue0138886
00:34:25.560 --> 00:34:28.560
The law has to be accessible to the people.

cue0138887
00:34:28.560 --> 00:34:31.560
The people have to learn the law.

cue0138888
00:34:31.560 --> 00:34:36.560
It has to be articulated in a manner

cue0138889
00:34:36.560 --> 00:34:43.560
that the common man could see the law by himself.

cue0138890
00:34:46.560 --> 00:34:50.560
And if we do not have such a system,

cue0138891
00:34:50.560 --> 00:34:58.560
then we're going to be a kind of hierarchy with a priesthood.

cue0138892
00:34:59.560 --> 00:35:05.560
Now, Christians have a hierarchy.

cue0138893
00:35:05.560 --> 00:35:10.560
But they have a vote with power.

cue0138894
00:35:10.560 --> 00:35:17.560
We Jews, we have a million votes but not one vote.

cue0138895
00:35:17.560 --> 00:35:21.560
It's the Rabbi.

cue0138896
00:35:21.560 --> 00:35:24.560
So how could this work?

cue0138897
00:35:25.560 --> 00:35:31.560
The genius of my managers was to produce a book

cue0138898
00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:41.560
that enables every single individual who knows how to translate the Bible properly,

cue0138899
00:35:41.560 --> 00:35:46.560
that was the equivalent of elementary education,

cue0138900
00:35:47.560 --> 00:35:54.560
to find any law and to decode it on his own.

cue0138901
00:35:56.560 --> 00:35:58.560
Mishne Tora, I counted.

cue0138902
00:35:58.560 --> 00:36:02.560
It counts a thousand chapters.

cue0138903
00:36:02.560 --> 00:36:05.560
And they are divided in the most meticulous way.

cue0138904
00:36:05.560 --> 00:36:11.560
I describe the Mishne Tora Bam Bam as a computer.

cue0138905
00:36:11.560 --> 00:36:14.560
It's a true computer with close references.

cue0138906
00:36:14.560 --> 00:36:20.560
One that you know how to manage to work out this computer.

cue0138907
00:36:20.560 --> 00:36:26.560
You could find any law in a matter of minutes.

cue0138908
00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:30.560
And what happened?

cue0138909
00:36:30.560 --> 00:36:33.560
It happened in this way.

cue0138910
00:36:33.560 --> 00:36:40.560
The Jew, the common Jew, was free from the clergy.

cue0138911
00:36:41.560 --> 00:36:53.560
A Jew did not depend on a system that was inaccessible to him.

cue0138912
00:36:54.560 --> 00:36:58.560
They say that my manager was an elitist.

cue0138913
00:36:58.560 --> 00:37:00.560
I disagree.

cue0138914
00:37:00.560 --> 00:37:06.560
I think that my manager was the man who cared more than anybody else

cue0138915
00:37:07.560 --> 00:37:17.560
about the Jews and the Sams and the Jacks and the Davids of Israel.

cue0138916
00:37:17.560 --> 00:37:25.560
And in this way, he gave the Jew self-autonomy.

cue0138917
00:37:25.560 --> 00:37:28.560
Yes, we respect the rabbis.

cue0138918
00:37:28.560 --> 00:37:30.560
We love the rabbis.

cue0138919
00:37:31.560 --> 00:37:38.560
Because, thank God, we have a book that enables us to appreciate them.

cue0138920
00:37:38.560 --> 00:37:42.560
And when they say something, we look into the good book of Aram Bam,

cue0138921
00:37:42.560 --> 00:37:46.560
and say, you know, the Rabbi is right.

cue0138922
00:37:46.560 --> 00:37:50.560
And this establishes a special bond.

cue0138923
00:37:50.560 --> 00:37:55.560
That was the contribution of my manager.

cue0138924
00:37:55.560 --> 00:37:57.560
We don't want slaves.

cue0138925
00:37:57.560 --> 00:38:07.560
We don't want people to be prisoners of an authoritarian system to which you have no access.

cue0138926
00:38:09.560 --> 00:38:11.560
And the Jew became indifferent.

cue0138927
00:38:13.560 --> 00:38:17.560
But, here's another point, a finer point.

cue0138928
00:38:17.560 --> 00:38:19.560
A finer point.

cue0138929
00:38:20.560 --> 00:38:22.560
A Jew.

cue0138930
00:38:26.560 --> 00:38:28.560
There are problems.

cue0138931
00:38:29.560 --> 00:38:39.560
As we grow, the answers that we got when we were children sometimes become problematic.

cue0138932
00:38:40.560 --> 00:38:46.560
As a matter of fact, they should become problematic if we grow.

cue0138933
00:38:48.560 --> 00:38:55.560
Growing, in a sense, means always more questions.

cue0138934
00:38:57.560 --> 00:39:04.560
And therefore, part of human nature is to become perplexed.

cue0138935
00:39:05.560 --> 00:39:12.560
Perplexity is not for unthinking people.

cue0138936
00:39:14.560 --> 00:39:19.560
It's for anybody who is not afraid to grow.

cue0138937
00:39:20.560 --> 00:39:26.560
If you're not perplexed, if you don't make wonders,

cue0138938
00:39:27.560 --> 00:39:34.560
you're not growing. You're cheating yourself.

cue0138939
00:39:36.560 --> 00:39:41.560
And my mother wrote the famous Guide to the Perplexed.

cue0138940
00:39:42.560 --> 00:39:48.560
To be Jewish, it means not to be ashamed to say, I don't know.

cue0138941
00:39:49.560 --> 00:39:51.560
My mother doesn't give answers.

cue0138942
00:39:51.560 --> 00:39:54.560
The answers that my mother gives are few.

cue0138943
00:39:55.560 --> 00:40:00.560
